WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:03.830
Narrator: This is a scene
from "The Amazing Spider-Man."

00:00:03.830 --> 00:00:06.700
This is from Marvel's "Luke Cage."

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:08.750
And this is a scene from "Joker."

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:10.160
Notice something similar?

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:13.260
They're all shot in the exact same place:

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:15.660
the 12th Avenue underpass in Harlem.

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:18.010
Samson Jacobson: When you stand
underneath Riverside Drive,

00:00:18.010 --> 00:00:22.210
I mean, it is the classic
abandoned New York location

00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:23.780
that everybody always wants.

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:26.080
Narrator: The underpass
is known for its viaduct,

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:28.160
an arching structure with a grandeur

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:29.860
filmmakers are drawn to.

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:31.060
Aaron Hurvitz: It gets a lot of play

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:34.820
because it is just a really
visually captivating space.

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:38.350
It gives this basically cascading overhang

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:40.330
of just metalwork.

00:00:40.330 --> 00:00:41.270
Narrator: This is just one

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:43.220
of several New York filming locations

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:46.890
you see pop up again and
again in shows and movies.

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:48.880
We spoke with five location scouts

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:52.133
to find out why these spots
are in such high demand.

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:55.350
If you look closely at the prison scenes

00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:57.280
in a bunch of New York movies,

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:59.220
you'll see these were all filmed

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.920
at Arthur Kill Correctional Facility.

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:04.080
This decommissioned
prison in Staten Island

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:06.580
has everything you could
ever need for a jail

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:08.810
or prison scene, from the barbed wire

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:12.100
and guard tower in front
to real cells inside.

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:16.230
It also comes with a lot of
space, with 70 acres of grounds

00:01:16.230 --> 00:01:18.690
and these long stretches of hallway.

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:21.320
That helps for choreographing
action sequences.

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:22.770
It's that kind of facility,

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:24.700
and there aren't that
many, that lends itself

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:27.670
to having these really
long, unbroken takes,

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:29.620
like "Daredevil" season three.

00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:31.736
12 or 13 minutes uncut

00:01:31.736 --> 00:01:34.330
just moving through the entire space,

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:38.420
down the hallways, past the
cells, out to the front door.

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:40.910
Narrator: The location is
surprisingly versatile.

00:01:40.910 --> 00:01:43.010
Malaika: Multiple cellblocks can look

00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:46.050
like different prisons if you
didn't want to just be at one.

00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:47.842
Bruno Barros: There are
sections that could work

00:01:47.842 --> 00:01:49.535
as a hospital, and it's great because,

00:01:49.535 --> 00:01:52.127
let's say you have to marry
two locations together

00:01:52.127 --> 00:01:55.080
and those scenes don't fill a full day.

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:57.730
You can combine them into one day

00:01:57.730 --> 00:01:59.060
and film it there, for example.

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:00.720
Narrator: The facility's giant parking lot

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:03.737
was even dressed up as
Afghanistan for "The Code."

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:07.590
Dramatic mystery scenes,
villain face-offs,

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:11.430
and wild car stunts have
all used this very backdrop:

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:15.330
the Brooklyn Navy Yard, a
vestige of industrial New York.

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:18.530
Brooklyn once had a busy
industrial waterfront,

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:20.070
but over the past few decades,

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:22.620
high-rise apartment complexes
and office buildings

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:25.430
have moved in and turned
the former manufacturing hub

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:26.960
much more residential.

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:30.080
That's why scouts return again
and again to this shipyard,

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:32.280
where 70,000 people once worked

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:35.100
before it closed down in 1966.

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:37.760
When everything is emptied
out, the abandoned docks

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:40.740
and hulking warehouses can
create an ominous look,

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:43.060
exactly what crime shows are after.

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:45.427
You want that, you know,
really industrial or,

00:02:45.427 --> 00:02:47.930
"Oh, no, somebody's getting
murdered on the docks at night!"

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:49.140
Great place to go to.

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:49.990
Narrator: Bodies have been dumped

00:02:49.990 --> 00:02:52.470
at this dry dock in multiple shows.

00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:54.700
The amount of open space makes it possible

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:56.610
to execute wild stunts,

00:02:56.610 --> 00:02:59.100
like when "Daredevil" blew
up a boat on the docks.

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:01.010
Malaika: The end of the
first "John Wick" movie,

00:03:01.010 --> 00:03:02.110
there was a car chase,

00:03:02.110 --> 00:03:05.184
and then a car crashed into this dry dock.

00:03:05.184 --> 00:03:07.487
You know, dropped into it.

00:03:07.487 --> 00:03:10.768
Narrator: Many filmmakers
focus on the grit of the city,

00:03:10.768 --> 00:03:12.375
but some scenes call for a more

00:03:12.375 --> 00:03:14.040
buttoned-up New York setting,

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:15.940
like this one that pops up again and again

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:18.760
in scenes involving business
or government meetings.

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:20.970
Manhattan has plenty of office spaces,

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:23.760
but many are on extremely high floors,

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:26.810
which means the lighting
very much depends on the sky.

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:29.280
Rather than relying on natural
light from the windows,

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:31.770
the filmmakers need to
control the lighting.

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:35.520
Then you can basically shoot
it any which way you want

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:38.010
and have it look always the same.

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:39.770
Narrator: But the higher
the office spaces are,

00:03:39.770 --> 00:03:41.840
the more difficult to get lights into.

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:43.840
That's what makes this spot prime.

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:45.480
It's an office on the third floor

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:46.970
of the Brooklyn Public Library

00:03:46.970 --> 00:03:49.800
with big windows that crews
can pass lights through,

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:51.440
and because it's on a reachable floor,

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:54.620
they can do it without getting
a big Condor lift involved.

00:03:54.620 --> 00:03:57.490
The office also has a
versatile mid-century design

00:03:57.490 --> 00:03:59.450
that can work across many decades,

00:03:59.450 --> 00:04:01.720
from modern business
dramas like "Billions"

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:04.677
to period pieces like "The
Marvelous Mrs. Maisel."

00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:08.270
A lot of the most famous
subway scenes are shot

00:04:08.270 --> 00:04:12.210
at one specific station:
Hoyt-Schermerhorn.

00:04:12.210 --> 00:04:14.370
The reason this spot is so popular?

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:16.410
Well, filming authentic subway scenes

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:18.410
comes with huge challenges.

00:04:18.410 --> 00:04:21.120
Sometimes you're at the whim of the MTA,

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:23.220
and things can change.

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:25.440
We had that happen for "In the Heights."

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:28.403
They had to reroute their trash trains,

00:04:28.403 --> 00:04:29.803
and essentially their trash trains

00:04:29.803 --> 00:04:31.494
had to come through our set.

00:04:31.494 --> 00:04:32.850
Narrator: Crews are less likely to run

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:34.930
into these logistical
problems if they film

00:04:34.930 --> 00:04:36.920
at tracks that are no longer in use,

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:38.900
like the one at Hoyt-Schermerhorn.

00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:40.148
It's a working station,

00:04:40.148 --> 00:04:43.740
but with one deactivated
platform among three active ones.

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:45.540
It's a place where we know we can film

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:47.125
during regular business hours.

00:04:47.125 --> 00:04:48.378
Narrator: Signage can turn it into

00:04:48.378 --> 00:04:50.420
basically any other subway stop,

00:04:50.420 --> 00:04:52.770
like in this scene from
"Coming to America."

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:54.220
When Lisa enters the station,

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:56.370
it's labeled as Van Wyck Boulevard,

00:04:56.370 --> 00:04:59.850
but if you look closely,
you'll see it's actually Hoyt.

00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:02.150
The giveaway is those
blue and yellow tiles

00:05:02.150 --> 00:05:04.008
with a circled L in the middle,

00:05:04.008 --> 00:05:05.241
remnants of what was once

00:05:05.241 --> 00:05:07.527
one of Brooklyn's biggest
department stores,

00:05:07.527 --> 00:05:10.160
Loeser's, which stood
where Hoyt does today.

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:11.830
Then, when Lisa exits the train,

00:05:11.830 --> 00:05:13.620
the station's labeled Sutphin,

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:15.993
but this was also filmed at Hoyt.

00:05:17.140 --> 00:05:18.440
Recognize this bar?

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:21.540
It's appeared in everything
from TV shows like "Gotham"

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:23.330
to movies like "The Kitchen."

00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:26.810
For scenes set in dive bars
and shadowy back rooms,

00:05:26.810 --> 00:05:30.960
directors like Martin Scorsese
often want the real deal.

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:33.760
Brooklyn's Irish Haven
has kept its tile floors

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:37.820
and wood paneling mostly
unchanged since 1964,

00:05:37.820 --> 00:05:40.110
when it was a favorite
spot for ironworkers

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:41.810
to unwind after work.

00:05:41.810 --> 00:05:44.767
There's a pool table,
dartboard, and jukebox.

00:05:44.767 --> 00:05:48.240
It has also just this great,
seasoned New York look to it.

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:50.170
You don't have to change
too much when you go there.

00:05:50.170 --> 00:05:53.330
Narrator: The no-frills
exterior and dimly lit interior

00:05:53.330 --> 00:05:56.370
are well suited to gritty
settings like Gotham,

00:05:56.370 --> 00:05:58.770
and the classically divey
setting can stand in

00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:01.380
for other cities, too, not just New York.

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:04.110
The bar and pool table are
famous for these scenes

00:06:04.110 --> 00:06:06.940
in the Boston-set "The Departed."

00:06:06.940 --> 00:06:08.650
When you see New York in a movie,

00:06:08.650 --> 00:06:12.360
you might see a scene like this or this:

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:15.400
a dark, seedy alleyway
filled with graffiti,

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:19.400
shuttered apartment windows,
and old fire escapes.

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:22.730
But in reality, New York
doesn't really have alleyways,

00:06:22.730 --> 00:06:25.720
which means dozens of famous
scenes have been filmed

00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:29.530
in this exact spot to
get that gritty look.

00:06:29.530 --> 00:06:32.070
One reason so many scripts
call for New York scenes

00:06:32.070 --> 00:06:34.960
to take place in alleys
is, well, Hollywood.

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:38.370
Aaron: The writing for most
projects takes place in LA.

00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:41.500
When you go to LA,
there's alleys everywhere.

00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:42.940
Narrator: New York was designed according

00:06:42.940 --> 00:06:46.110
to the grid system, with
little to no space for alleys.

00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:48.050
The real estate is so valuable

00:06:48.050 --> 00:06:50.240
that we just didn't leave space for it.

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:52.858
Samson: If they're envisioning LA alleys,

00:06:52.858 --> 00:06:54.310
probably not gonna find them.

00:06:54.310 --> 00:06:57.170
But there are other types of alleys

00:06:57.170 --> 00:06:58.630
that are smaller and tighter.

00:06:58.630 --> 00:07:00.840
Narrator: Like Cortlandt
Alley, which might be

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:04.260
the most used filming
location in all of Manhattan.

00:07:04.260 --> 00:07:07.070
Eric: That is an alley that
gives you just about everything.

00:07:07.070 --> 00:07:09.400
It's fire escapes, it's graffiti.

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:10.625
Narrator: Many of the apartment windows

00:07:10.625 --> 00:07:12.850
have these big, decades-old shutters,

00:07:12.850 --> 00:07:14.710
which adds to the alley's aesthetic.

00:07:14.710 --> 00:07:16.520
Crews can put lights inside the windows

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:17.730
of the apartments above.

00:07:17.730 --> 00:07:20.560
I've paid people in the
same apartment buildings

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:23.850
for lighting positions looking
in the same exact spots that,

00:07:23.850 --> 00:07:27.210
you know, "Teenage Mutant
Ninja Turtles" shot in as well.

00:07:27.210 --> 00:07:28.953
Narrator: The location's use goes beyond

00:07:28.953 --> 00:07:30.430
just gritty scenes.

00:07:30.430 --> 00:07:33.970
Take this wholesome scene in
"Annie," which Eric worked on.

00:07:33.970 --> 00:07:35.300
The team used Cortlandt Alley

00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:37.520
to create a path for this chase sequence,

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:40.220
adding trash and boxes as obstacles.

00:07:40.220 --> 00:07:42.450
Little nooks in the
alleyway served as spots

00:07:42.450 --> 00:07:44.950
for the dog trainers
to hide during filming.

00:07:44.950 --> 00:07:47.360
Since Cortlandt Alley
is owned by the city,

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:49.050
it's cheaper to film there.

00:07:49.050 --> 00:07:52.840
As it's one of the very
few actual public alleys,

00:07:52.840 --> 00:07:54.150
it gets used all the time.

00:07:54.150 --> 00:07:55.610
Narrator: So much that sometimes

00:07:55.610 --> 00:07:57.870
it's put in what's called the "hot zone."

00:07:57.870 --> 00:08:00.280
In New York City, when
something's been filmed too much

00:08:00.280 --> 00:08:04.180
and the residents are unhappy
with the impact it has,

00:08:04.180 --> 00:08:06.610
the mayor's office will put
something on the hot zone,

00:08:06.610 --> 00:08:08.710
and that means that there's
a sort of moratorium

00:08:08.710 --> 00:08:11.360
on filming until they take it off there.

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:13.012
Narrator: Cortlandt
Alley has stayed looking

00:08:13.012 --> 00:08:15.145
more or less the same for decades,

00:08:15.145 --> 00:08:18.330
but in much of New York,
things are always changing.

00:08:18.330 --> 00:08:19.570
These steps in "Joker"?

00:08:19.570 --> 00:08:22.130
They were actually the
director's second choice.

00:08:22.130 --> 00:08:24.050
Todd Phillips initially requested

00:08:24.050 --> 00:08:27.230
this menacing set of stairs
from "American Gangster."

00:08:27.230 --> 00:08:29.738
And when I went to go visit it,

00:08:29.738 --> 00:08:32.620
it had gotten a whole makeover.

00:08:32.620 --> 00:08:35.400
Brand-new concrete, little cute trees.

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:36.820
It had been power-washed

00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:38.900
of all of its graffiti and grit.

00:08:38.900 --> 00:08:42.240
Narrator: After visiting 25
to 30 different step streets,

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:44.060
Aaron tracked down this one,

00:08:44.060 --> 00:08:46.900
which had exactly what
they were looking for.

00:08:46.900 --> 00:08:48.000
It brought drama.

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:51.090
Aaron: In terms of the length
and steepness of the stairs,

00:08:51.090 --> 00:08:54.960
kind of confined and narrow,
it hit all the marks,

00:08:54.960 --> 00:08:56.735
and it had that great view

00:08:56.735 --> 00:08:59.835
just off from the top
looking into the city

00:08:59.835 --> 00:09:02.600
that really worked for us as well.

00:09:02.600 --> 00:09:05.460
Narrator: The spot
became instantly iconic,

00:09:05.460 --> 00:09:09.420
showing that while the face of
the city is always changing,

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:13.603
New York will always be a
great place to shoot movies.

